🧠 Core Concepts
Linux Filesystem Hierarchy
Linux uses a single inverted‑tree structure. Everything starts at / (root).

Directory	Purpose
/	Root of the entire filesystem
/etc	System‑wide configuration files (e.g., /etc/passwd, /etc/nginx/)
/var	Variable data — logs, spools, caches (/var/log, /var/lib)
/home	User home directories (/home/alice)
/tmp	Temporary files; cleared on reboot
/opt	Optional/add‑on software packages (e.g., /opt/google)
/usr	User system resources — binaries, libraries, docs (/usr/bin, /usr/lib)
/proc	Virtual filesystem for process and kernel info (/proc/cpuinfo)
/sys	Virtual filesystem for hardware and kernel parameters
Tip: man hier gives the full official description.

File Commands
Command	Description	Example
ls	List directory contents	ls -lah /var/log
cd	Change directory	cd /etc/nginx
pwd	Print working directory	pwd
cp	Copy files/dirs	cp file.txt /tmp/
mv	Move/rename	mv old.txt new.txt
rm	Remove files/dirs	rm -rf /tmp/junk
find	Search for files	find / -name "*.conf"
grep	Search text	grep "error" /var/log/syslog
head	First lines	head -n 20 file.log
tail	Last lines	tail -f /var/log/syslog
less	Page through file	less /etc/services
Text Processing Commands
Command	Description	Example
awk	Pattern scanning & processing	awk '{print $1}' file.txt
sed	Stream editor	sed 's/old/new/g' file.txt
sort	Sort lines	sort -n numbers.txt
cut	Extract columns	cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd
xargs	Build command lines from input	find . -name "*.tmp" | xargs rm
🛠 Command Reference
Navigation & Listing
bash
pwd                       # Show current directory
cd /var/log               # Change to /var/log
cd ~                      # Go to home directory
cd -                      # Go to previous directory
ls -l                     # Long listing
ls -a                     # Show hidden files
ls -lh                    # Human-readable sizes
ls -lt                    # Sort by modification time
File Manipulation
bash
cp file1 file2            # Copy file
cp -r dir1 dir2           # Copy directory recursively
mv file1 /tmp/            # Move file
mv oldname newname        # Rename
rm file                   # Remove file
rm -r dir                 # Remove directory recursively
rm -rf dir                # Force remove (use with caution!)
Searching
bash
# find
find /etc -name "*.conf"                  # By name
find /var -type f -size +100M             # Files larger than 100MB
find /home -user alice                    # Files owned by user
find . -mtime -7                          # Modified in last 7 days

# grep
grep "error" /var/log/syslog              # Basic search
grep -i "error" file.txt                  # Case-insensitive
grep -r "TODO" .                          # Recursive
grep -v "debug" file.log                  # Invert match
grep -E "error|fail" file.log             # Extended regex
Viewing Files
bash
head -n 20 file.log       # First 20 lines
tail -n 50 file.log       # Last 50 lines
tail -f file.log          # Follow (live)
less file.log             # Scrollable viewer (q to quit)
Text Processing
bash
awk '{print $1, $3}' file.txt             # Print columns 1 and 3
awk -F: '{print $1}' /etc/passwd          # Custom delimiter
sed 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt                # Replace all
sed -i 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt             # In-place edit
sort file.txt                             # Alphabetical
sort -n numbers.txt                       # Numeric
sort -k2 file.txt                         # By second column
cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd                   # First field, colon delimiter
cut -c1-10 file.txt                       # First 10 characters
find . -name "*.log" | xargs rm           # Delete all .log files
🧪 Lab: Build the devops-lab Structure
Objective: Create a well‑organised project skeleton and populate it with sample files to practice commands.

Step 1: Create the directory tree
bash
mkdir -p devops-lab/{scripts,logs,configs,backup}
cd devops-lab
touch README.md
Verify:

bash
tree devops-lab     # if tree is installed
# or
find devops-lab -type d
Step 2: Add sample files
bash
# scripts
echo '#!/bin/bash' > scripts/hello.sh
echo 'echo "Hello, DevOps"' >> scripts/hello.sh
chmod +x scripts/hello.sh

# logs
for i in {1..5}; do echo "Log entry $i" > logs/app-$i.log; done

# configs
echo "server_name=localhost" > configs/app.conf
echo "port=8080" >> configs/app.conf

# backup
echo "backup placeholder" > backup/backup.txt
Step 3: Practice navigation and listing
bash
ls -R
cd scripts && pwd && cd ..
Step 4: Document the structure in README.md
bash
cat > README.md <<'EOF'
# DevOps Lab

A structured workspace for Linux and DevOps practice.

## Directory layout

- `scripts/` – executable scripts
- `logs/` – application log files
- `configs/` – configuration files
- `backup/` – backup artefacts
EOF
Step 5: Clean up (optional)
bash
cd ..
rm -rf devops-lab
🔍 Practice: find, grep, awk, sed, sort, cut, xargs
Using the devops-lab structure (recreate if needed), run these exercises.

find
bash
# Find all .log files
find devops-lab -name "*.log"

# Find files larger than 1KB
find devops-lab -type f -size +1k

# Find and delete all .tmp files (if any)
find devops-lab -name "*.tmp" -delete
grep
bash
# Search for "port" in configs
grep -r "port" devops-lab/configs

# Case-insensitive search
grep -i "hello" devops-lab/scripts/hello.sh

# Show line numbers
grep -n "server" devops-lab/configs/app.conf
awk
bash
# Print first column of app.conf (key=value pairs)
awk -F= '{print $1}' devops-lab/configs/app.conf

# Print lines with port > 8000
awk -F= '$1=="port" && $2>8000 {print}' devops-lab/configs/app.conf
sed
bash
# Replace "localhost" with "127.0.0.1" in app.conf
sed 's/localhost/127.0.0.1/' devops-lab/configs/app.conf

# In-place edit (backup original)
sed -i.bak 's/localhost/127.0.0.1/' devops-lab/configs/app.conf
sort
bash
# Sort log files by name
ls devops-lab/logs | sort

# Numeric sort
echo -e "10\n2\n30" | sort -n
cut
bash
# Extract usernames from /etc/passwd
cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd | head -5

# Extract first 5 characters of each line in app.conf
cut -c1-5 devops-lab/configs/app.conf
xargs
bash
# Remove all .log files using xargs
find devops-lab/logs -name "*.log" | xargs rm

# Echo each file
find devops-lab -type f | xargs -I {} echo "File: {}"
⚠️ Common Pitfalls
rm -rf / – never run this. Always double‑check paths.

find with -exec – remember {} and \; or +.

grep -r on / – can be extremely slow; limit scope.

sed -i – always test without -i first, or use a backup suffix.

xargs with spaces in filenames – use -0 with find -print0.

